you can't tell the story of the American  jury without telling the story of  America it starts with the Declaration  of Independence in which we as a legal  matter define ourselves as an  independent people and assert our  sovereignty as a people that that is the  Declaration of Independence we don't get  to the jury until we get to the  Constitution of the state in the  constitution of the state the jury is  the method that we the people adopted as  our strategy for controlling the state  the most fundamental relationship  between citizen and state was defined by  the jury the jury took two forms the  core idea is one of justice and the core  idea of justice is fair trial and a fair  trial has three parts a fair charge a  fair opportunity to defend and a fair  verdict all right now the jury is the  linchpin of fair trial first with  respect to the charge the grand jury the  idea was we the people should control  what charge can be brought against a  person that can put him in such jeopardy  before a jury of his peers that they  could take his freedom away that's the  grand jury and then the opportunity to  defend that becomes cross-examination  the high point of the trial the actual  testing of the witnesses who have made  the charge and then the verdict and  that's the Pettit jury  the idea of 12 coming to a unanimous  verdict and that's the idea of the jury  and the thought is that if the state  couldn't take a man's freedom away  unless he had a fair trial the jury was  the bulwark of freedom that's the story  of the journey
 

